# homma-api

The vocabulary every other crate speaks. Types and the traits over them, and no
I/O of any kind.

## A record

Everything homma stores is a record. A message, a task, a delivery cursor, an
entry in a monitoring log: one shape, distinguished by what its kind says about
it.

Three fields are required of every record, because homma's own machinery reads
them.

**`id`** addresses it. **`kind`** names the declaration that governs it.
**`provenance`** is a reference to whoever created it, and the standing of a
record is derived from what that reference points at rather than stored beside it:
a person's own reference is ratified, an agent's is agent output, a forge's is
mapped and read-only. Storing the standing as well would let the two disagree,
which is the whole reason it is derived.

Everything else a record carries is declared by its kind.

## A kind

A kind is configuration, not code. It names the attributes a record of that kind
has, their types, which are required, and whether records of that kind may be
edited after they are written.

That last flag is what lets correspondence and structural work share one engine.
Messages declare themselves append-only and are never edited; tasks declare
themselves mutable and change as work moves. Nothing about the store knows which
is which, and a workspace that wants a third behaviour declares a third kind.

## Attribute types

Present, because something uses each:

**`text`**, with a hint saying whether it is plain or carries markup.
**`instant`**, a point in time. **`ref<ns>`**, a reference into a namespace, which
is the one type that collapses several: a handle is a reference into the
registry's namespace, a link to another record is a reference into that record's
kind, and a forge reference is a reference into a forge's.

Deliberately absent, and each for a reason rather than an oversight: a date
distinct from an instant, a duration, a number, a decimal with declared precision,
a recurrence, a fractional rank, and an ordered workflow with terminal and
off-path states. Each is designed and none is needed by the first records that
exist. Kinds being configuration means every one of them is additive when it
arrives, so building them ahead of a consumer would fix a shape nothing has
pushed on.

## Configuration

**No key is required.** The content repository defaults to `local`, meaning the
configuration's own directory, which homma initialises if it is not a repository
yet; a git URI names one elsewhere. Every path likewise has a default homma will
create and an override for a workspace whose convention differs, so a directory
holding an empty registry is already a workspace.
`[workspace] name` is one plain path component, refused otherwise, since the
role file of a session lives in a directory of that name.
`[session]` says how a session is told apart: `cloud_var`, the environment
variable that is `1` in a cloud session. Unset, it keeps the variable homma has
read until now, as `[auth] service_prefix` keeps its keychain prefix, until the
manifests that rely on the old values set them and both defaults become neutral.
`keys_of` lists every key a deserialisable type reads, with no manifest: it
deserialises the type from a probe that records the field names serde asks for
and descends into each field's value, writing a map's keys `*` and an array's
elements under the array's own name. A field read as any value, a raw
`toml::Value` or an untagged enum, shows no shape, so `Keys` holds such keys
apart from the rest as opaque, and a caller can graft in the type that reads
them.
A key under `[paths]` that `Paths` does not read is kept by name in
`paths_unread` on the workspace: the raw table's keys that the key probe of
`Paths` does not list. Nothing is refused for it yet, so a manifest whose other
programs keep keys there goes on working while they move them out; a later
release refuses such a key as every other table does.
Identities are blocks: a role, a handle, and whatever that role needs. A person
needs a git identity and a workspace; a consultant needs neither.

## A skill's effort and a mode

`Effort` is the host's fixed vocabulary for the effort of a turn, `low`, `medium`,
`high`, `xhigh` and `max`, read from its word and refused on any other.
`ModeMeta` is what a mode declares: its `type`, its `effort`, `when` it is reached
for, the `skills` and `tools` it names, and whether it `ends_turn`. `mode::parse`
reads it off a mode file's frontmatter, refusing an unknown key, a missing one, a
list not written inline and an `ends_turn` that is not `true` or `false`.

## What the git contract promises

**Every path handed to it is an `AbsPath`, a type that cannot be relative.** A
relative path resolves against whatever directory the process happens to be in,
so a guard walking upward from one walks up from the process's directory and
passes, and a clone target that is one lands wherever the operator was standing.

**Descending and anchoring are different operations and only one may leave.**

*Anchoring* takes a path the caller is entitled to put anywhere. A workspace
legitimately lives outside the content repository, so a relative one going up is
correct, and what guards it is a containment check against the filesystem.

*Descending* walks into a tree we own: a repository's own `.git`, a handle's
directory, a definition's filename. **Nothing descended into has any business
leaving**, so it cannot: a parent component clamps at the receiver rather than at
the filesystem root. Every caller passes a literal, and none of them was trying
to leave.

Sharing one method between the two is how a configured path reached another
repository's tree. The configured paths additionally carry a type that refuses an
absolute or escaping value **when the configuration is parsed**, so the failure is
reported where somebody can act on it rather than where a file lands.

**What the type carries is absoluteness, and nothing else.** It does not carry
containment: `/a/b/../../etc` is absolute, and so is a path pointing straight
into somebody else's repository. Containment is a question about the filesystem
rather than about the value, so it stays a runtime check, and the check belongs
on **the path being written to** rather than on the one it was resolved against.
A round that put the type in place and left that check on the wrong path shipped
the same escape it was closing.

**The type is the point, and prose was tried first.** Stating the precondition in
a comment means re-checking it at every call. Checking it at runtime in one
implementation leaves the other six unchecked, which is what happened: three
consecutive rounds each closed the route they were shown and left the next one
open, and the runtime check that was added turned out to be unreachable in both
fakes. A precondition in the signature is checked once, by the compiler, for every
caller that will ever exist.

**Absence is `None`, not an error.** Asked what a remote points at, a directory
that is not a repository points at nothing; that is an answer, not a failure. An
error means a repository that exists and cannot be read. Collapsing the two makes
the ordinary bootstrap, a directory with a registry and a remote to clone from,
fail outright.

## Mapped and staffed

An identity whose role owns a workspace is in one of two states, and they are not
degrees of completeness.

**Staffed** means it has a workspace on disk, a git identity, and repositories. It
is somebody who works.

**Mapped** means the entry records that a boundary has been drawn and who owns it,
so the same ground is not carved twice by somebody arriving later. It has no
workspace and is not supposed to have one.

Without the distinction the two are indistinguishable, because a mapped entry
lacks exactly the fields an unfinished one lacks. A registry holding a dozen
mapped entries then reports a dozen broken ones, and a report that is wrong a
dozen times is a report nobody reads.

**The staffing state is declared, never inferred from an absent workspace.** Inference is
cheaper and wrong: an entry whose workspace was mistyped or dropped would read as
a deliberate decision, which is the confusion the distinction exists to remove. So
an entry says it is staffed, and a staffed entry lacking its workspace is still a
gap and still reported.

## The catalogue

The `[org.*]` rows are the one catalogue of who exists. Nothing else in a
workspace lists an agent's name or address; a file that needs them is rendered
from these rows.

**Five roles.** The king is the human. A hand is a standing agent with a domain,
a workspace and a memory. An expert is dispatched and never resident. A
journeyman is the lighter identity a hand's team reserves for a while: a name, an
address and a few lines of temperament, no workspace and no memory of its own. A
general is labour with no persona at all. The roles are homma's vocabulary and
fixed here; what a workspace calls its participants is its own.

**An entry may carry `aliases`**, further handles that reach the same participant,
for a nickname beside a name or a former title. A handle and an alias are one
namespace: no two entries share either.

**A handle is a name, checked as one.** It becomes a directory name and a file
name, so it may not be empty, be `.` or `..`, start with a dot, or carry a path
separator or a control character. Letters of any script, digits, `-`, `_` and `.`
are allowed, so a roster written in Finnish needs no transliteration.

**No two entries share an address.** Author and committer addresses are compared
the way the matcher compares them, lowercased with any plus tag stripped, so two
entries cannot both answer to one address and a lookup never picks one silently.

## Offices

An office is a standing job with a name everybody pings: the primary coordinator,
the tooling track. Its holder is a person of the catalogue, and the two are kept
apart: a commit carries the person, the channel and the tickets address the office,
and a holder can change without either moving.

**`[offices.<name>]` rows sit beside the `[org.*]` rows**, each an `Office` with an
optional `holder`, a handle or an alias of the catalogue, and `counted`, true
unless the office stands outside the count of coordinator slots. An office without a holder is one that exists and waits for its
person. A TOML key cannot repeat, so one office has at most one holder by
construction; the check refuses one handle holding two offices, a holder the
catalogue does not hold, and an office named like a handle or an alias, since both
are addressed by name on the channel.

## Matching an address

An address is an agent's when an entry other than the king's carries it as its
author or committer address. The lookup lowercases the address and strips a plus
tag from its local part before comparing, because a sub-agent commits as its
launcher's address with a tag naming it: `arlen+review@example.invalid` is Abe's.
Everything else is somebody who is not a catalogued agent, which is a human unless
another check says otherwise.

## Where the files are

`Paths` names two more places. **`identities`**, default `.shared/.index/identities.tsv`,
is the rendered index of every agent address. **`leases`**, default
`.shared/state/leases`, is the directory of lease records, one file per identity.
`SearchConfig`, the `[search]` table, names where `homma search` finds the
indexes: `members`, false unless set, `corpus_config`, `corpus_index` and
`member_index`, with the defaults the command surface gives; and the commands
an index not built is named with, `corpus_build`, `muisti index` by default and
run in the corpus's directory, and `member_build`, `cargo mock search --refresh`
by default and run in the member's clone.
`SummaryConfig`, the `[summary]` table, turns on the summarising pass and says
what makes its call: `enabled`, false unless set, `command`, `claude`, and
`model`, `haiku`.
`AskConfig`, the `[ask]` table, bounds what `homma ask` keeps: `cache_expire_hours`,
336, the hours after its last read an entry expires, and `cache_max_bytes`, 64 MiB,
the size the cache is cut back under, least recently read first. It refuses a key
it does not know.
`LeaseConfig`, the `[lease]` table, names the remote and the branch the records
live on and how many minutes a lease lasts before it lapses: `origin`, `main` and
120 unless set.
